WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.860
How does a kinase know that a lipid has just been cut, here and now?

2
00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:12.280
A receptor fires at the cell surface, and an enzyme cuts a lipid in the membrane.

3
00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:14.480
The kinase waits in the cytosol.

4
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:16.180
What could tell it?

5
00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:22.483
The lipid is PIP2, on the inside surface of the membrane.

6
00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:27.563
The enzyme phospholipase C cuts it, like a ticket torn along its perforation.

7
00:00:28.223 --> 00:00:33.163
One half is IP3, with three phosphates, so it dissolves in water and leaves.

8
00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:38.403
The other half is DAG, an oil, so it stays in the membrane where it was made.

9
00:00:38.943 --> 00:00:41.923
One cut makes two second messengers, in two places.

10
00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:45.787
Follow the half that left.

11
00:00:46.667 --> 00:00:51.887
IP3 diffuses to the endoplasmic reticulum, a calcium store inside the cell.

12
00:00:52.487 --> 00:00:55.627
A channel in its membrane opens when IP3 binds.

13
00:00:56.167 --> 00:01:01.227
The store holds free calcium at a thousand times the resting cytosolic level, or

14
00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:06.887
more. So an open channel means a fast rise, and DAG stays in the membrane.

15
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:13.140
Now meet the conventional kind of protein kinase C, which puts phosphates on serines

16
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.160
and threonines. At rest it is folded shut in the cytosol.

17
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.800
A stretch called the pseudosubstrate sits in its active site and blocks it.

18
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:26.240
The C2 part lands on the membrane only when it holds calcium.

19
00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.580
The C1 part binds DAG in the membrane.

20
00:01:30.683 --> 00:01:33.283
Each signal alone rises for many reasons.

21
00:01:33.943 --> 00:01:36.823
Calcium also rises when a voltage -gated channel opens.

22
00:01:37.443 --> 00:01:43.443
With calcium alone, C2 holds the membrane weakly, and the kinase stays largely off.

23
00:01:44.043 --> 00:01:46.563
Other lipid enzymes also make DAG.

24
00:01:46.563 --> 00:01:52.323
With DAG alone, C1 holds weakly too, and the kinase stays largely off.

25
00:01:54.683 --> 00:01:59.963
Each contact is weak alone, but binding energies add, so preferences multiply.

26
00:02:00.683 --> 00:02:05.983
Say each contact alone favors the membrane 30 -fold, and the pair favors it about

27
00:02:05.983 --> 00:02:12.183
900 -fold. Calcium and DAG appear together mainly because phospholipase C has just

28
00:02:12.183 --> 00:02:18.223
cut PIP2 there. Only then is the kinase held firmly enough to free its active site,

29
00:02:18.623 --> 00:02:20.643
so it works as a coincidence detector.

30
00:02:21.103 --> 00:02:26.503
So keep this rule: conventional protein kinase C responds weakly to either signal

31
00:02:26.503 --> 00:02:30.663
alone, and strongly when calcium and DAG arrive together.
